## 5. Interview-Week Checklist

### Triggers

- fixed length k and contiguous;
- longest/shortest feasible contiguous region;
- sorted pair/triple or monotone boundary objective;
- in-place filtering or compaction;
- multiple sorted frontiers.

### Invariants

- exactly k items in the fixed window;
- variable-window state is valid at answer time;
- eliminated pairs cannot contain a better answer;
- processed prefix is final;
- next output is the smallest current frontier.

### Traps

- negative numbers;
- original indices after sorting;
- duplicate values;
- stale or zero frequency keys;
- integer overflow;
- writing over unread merge values;
- confusing top-k with k-way merge.

### Anchor problems

- Maximum Average Subarray I;
- Minimum Size Subarray Sum;
- Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters;
- Two Sum II;
- 3Sum;
- Move Zeroes;
- Merge Sorted Array.

## 6. Pattern Mastery Checklist

### Fixed-size window

- [ ] Recognize it within 30 seconds.
- [ ] State the exact length invariant.
- [ ] Write the entering/leaving update.
- [ ] Explain running sum versus prefix sum.
- [ ] Handle frequency state.
- [ ] Explain complexity.

### Variable-size window

- [ ] State the feasibility condition.
- [ ] Prove monotonicity.
- [ ] Choose longest versus shortest loop order.
- [ ] Explain why the inner while is still linear.
- [ ] Identify when prefix sum or deque is required.
- [ ] Handle no-answer cases.

### Sorted opposite pointers

- [ ] State why sorting is safe.
- [ ] Prove each pointer movement.
- [ ] Preserve indices when needed.
- [ ] Handle duplicates in 3Sum.
- [ ] Track a best distance for closest variants.
- [ ] Distinguish hashmap Two Sum.

### Read/write pointers

- [ ] State what the output prefix means.
- [ ] Preserve order when required.
- [ ] Return the logical length.
- [ ] Distinguish compaction from partition.
- [ ] Justify O(1) extra space.

### Multi-sequence merge

- [ ] State what each source pointer represents.
- [ ] Merge two sources in linear time.
- [ ] Know when a heap is required.
- [ ] Distinguish frontier state from answer-candidate state.
- [ ] Handle exhausted sources and duplicates.

## 7. Active Recall Questions

1. What makes a sliding-window condition monotone?
2. Why does a fixed-size window need only one add and one remove?
3. Why can negative numbers break a sum-based variable window?
4. When is prefix sum plus hashmap better than a window?
5. Why does a too-small sorted pair sum force the left pointer to move?
6. Why does a too-large sorted pair sum force the right pointer to move?
7. Why is sorting unsafe when original indices must be returned?
8. Why must 3Sum skip duplicates at the fixed index?
9. What does the read/write prefix represent?
10. When can a partition use two pointers from opposite ends instead?
11. Why can two sorted arrays be merged without a heap?
12. Why does k-way merge usually need a heap?
13. What is the difference between a top-k heap and a frontier heap?
14. Why can a nested while loop still be O(n)?
15. What answer-update order is required for a shortest feasible window?
16. Which pattern would you choose for an exact subarray sum with negatives?
17. Which data structure maintains a sliding-window maximum?
18. What should you test when the window is empty?
19. What should you test when all values are duplicates?
20. What sentence proves that a pointer movement is safe?

## 8. Final Self-Test

For any new problem, answer these before coding:

~~~text
What is contiguous?
What is the objective?
What is the monotone property?
What state changes when a boundary moves?
What is my invariant?
What candidates become impossible after this move?
What is the complexity?
~~~

